From: Datel OS
Subject: load_cell: measure only while the samples are used

LoadCell subscribed to its sensor when Klipper became ready and never let
go, only to keep the averaged force in the status current. On the K1 that
is four HX711 at 80 samples per second, each sample converted and stored by
Python around the clock: with the printer idle, Klipper used 13.8 % of a
CPU, and more than half of that was this stream.

Subscribe to the sensor while something uses the samples (a tare, a probe,
a calibration, an API client of load_cell/dump_force) and for 5 s after, so
that a tare followed by a move, or the probes of a bed mesh, do not restart
it. A homing move has no client on the host, the probe on the MCU reads the
samples itself: home_start() holds the sensor on until home_wait() is over.

Starting the sensor before each use showed two things that the endless
stream had hidden after its first seconds:

- A stopped HX711 is powered down, and the bridge drifts while it warms up
  again: 18 g in 10 s on the K1 bed, 11 g of it in the first second. The
  samples of the first 2 s after a start are discarded and a collector
  waits for them, so the first tare of a sequence takes 2 s longer.

- FixedFreqReader started its clock regression when the measurements were
  requested, before the chips had woken up and settled. That first point
  was early, and the timestamps of the samples were late by 29 ms after the
  start and by more than 2 ms for 9 s, until it had decayed. A tap is
  located on the Z axis by those timestamps: at 3 mm/s, 29 ms are 0.09 mm,
  and eight probes right after a start drifted by 0.13 mm. A reader of
  such a chip now starts the regression at the first samples (4 ms after
  the start, and falling).

Probe repeatability on a K1, cold, eight samples each: standard deviation
0.010, 0.011 and 0.013 mm with the sensor started 2 s before each run,
0.014, 0.013 and 0.012 mm with the sensor running all the time.

The force values in the status are present only while the sensor measures.
Idle, Klipper now uses 3.3 % of a CPU.

